  {
    "text": "Clear All",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/?ea735457_page=10"
  },
  {
    "text": "Release PlanWhat is a Release Plan for product managers? Learn more about release plans and other product management terms in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/release-plan"
  },
  {
    "text": "RetentionCustomer retention refers to a company\u2019s or product\u2019s ability to retain customers over time. If a company or product has high customer retention, it means that customers return to purchase or continue using a product or service. If a company or product has low customer retention, it means that customers stop buying or using a product or service.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/retention"
  },
  {
    "text": "Retention RateWhy is the retention rate so important? It is used to determine the percentage of customers who continue paying for a product over time.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/retention-rate"
  },
  {
    "text": "RetrospectiveA retrospective is a meeting held after a product ships to discuss what happened during the product development and release process.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/retrospective"
  },
  {
    "text": "RICE Scoring ModelWhat is RICE Scoring Model? Learn more about RICE Scoring Model and other prioritization techniques in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/rice-scoring-model"
  },
  {
    "text": "RoadmapWhat is a Roadmap and why are they important for product managers? Learn more about product management terms in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/roadmap"
  },
  {
    "text": "Roadmap MilestonesA roadmap milestone is a date signaling an event or deadline that the product manager wants the team to be aware of.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/roadmap-milestones"
  },
  {
    "text": "Roadmapping ToolA roadmap is a strategic blueprint that captures and communicates the basic plan and goals for a project. A roadmapping tool can be used to create, update, and share this document, typically in a visual way.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/roadmapping-tool"
  },
  {
    "text": "Roadmap RevolutionA roadmap revolution is a complete re-evaluation of a product roadmap, commonly conducted at the beginning of the year.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/roadmap-revolution"
  },
  {
    "text": "Scaled Agile FrameworkAn overview of the Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) for product managers. Including pros and cons of this agile framework often used by large organizations.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scaled-agile-framework"
  },
  {
    "text": "Scope CreepScope creep is the phenomenon in which a team's initial plan slowly grows to include more goals, tasks, or requirements.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scope-creep"
  },
  {
    "text": "Scrum Agile FrameworkWhat is the scrum agile framework (SAFe)? Learn how teams can move projects forward in short-term blocks of work called sprints.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scrum-agile-framework"
  },
  {
    "text": "ScrumbanHow does scrumban work? Scrumban can help their teams focus on the correct strategic tasks while at the same time improving their processes.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scrumban"
  },
  {
    "text": "Scrum MasterDoes a scrum master do in agile team? Learn about the role, responsibilities, and training certifications of scrum masters.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scrum-master"
  },
  {
    "text": "Scrum MeetingA Scrum Meeting is a catch-all term for the various different meetings practiced by agile scrum teams. Learn more about agile in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/scrum-meeting"
  },
  {
    "text": "Service TransformationService transformation refers to the process of expanding an organization's focus to include new service offerings in addition to their product offering. When an organization decides to broaden their services portfolio, they not only bring more value to customers, they also open new streams of revenue outside of traditional product-generated revenue.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/service-transformation"
  },
  {
    "text": "Shadow ITShadow IT is a catch-all for any technology used within a corporate environment. The use of the technology typically outside the purview of the company\u2019s \u201cofficial\u201d information technology department.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/shadow-it"
  },
  {
    "text": "Shape Up MethodThe Shape Up Method describes the specific processes used by product development teams to shape, bet, and build meaningful products. It gives teams language and specific techniques to address the risks and unknowns at each stage of product development with the ultimate goal of shipping a great product on time.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/shape-up-method"
  },
  {
    "text": "Shipyard EngineA shipyard engine describes a product team\u2019s process to keep its organization informed about the frequent updates the team ships.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/shipyard-engine"
  },
  {
    "text": "SMART Goal SettingThe SMART framework provides the framework for setting clear, attainable goals in project management. The acronym stands for Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Relevant, and Time-bound.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/smart-goal-setting"
  },
  {
    "text": "SprintWhat is a sprint? Learn how agile sprints play a key role in the product development process and the pros and cons of using this framework.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/sprint"
  },
  {
    "text": "Sprint BacklogWhat is a sprint backlog? It's the set of items that a cross-functional product team selects from its product backlog to work on during the upcoming sprint.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/sprintbacklog"
  },
  {
    "text": "Sprint GoalWhat is a sprint goal? Learn how sprint goals play a role in the product development process and discover related topics in our glossary.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/sprint-goal"
  },
  {
    "text": "Sprint PlanningWhat is sprint planning? Learn more about sprint planning and other product development terminology in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/sprint-planning"
  },
  {
    "text": "StakeholderWhat is a stakeholder in a product management context? Learn about stakeholders and product management terminology in our resources library.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/stakeholder"
  },
  {
    "text": "Stakeholder AnalysisA stakeholder analysis can help you understand key people involved with a project. Read to learn how to conduct a stakeholder analysis.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/stakeholder-analysis"
  },
  {
    "text": "Stakeholder ManagementWhat is stakeholder management? Why is it key to product management? Learn how to identify your product\u2019s stakeholders in a few steps.",
    "url": "https://www.productplan.com/glossary/stakeholder-management"
  },
